{
  "_about": "ONE saved-document width-control upgrade, read by BOTH app/src/components/chart/engine/__tests__/savedDocumentUpgradeParity.test.js (memberDocumentMigration.migrateMemberDocument) and tests/test_saved_document_upgrade_parity.py (user_definitions.refresh_width_control_max). Each case starts from `base` (Uncharted Clouds as origin/master's door saved it before #513/#526: every plot width control {type:int,min:1,max:4,step:1}) and applies `ops` in order; `upgraded` lists the inputs whose `max` must become 10 (or `max[key]`); EVERY other byte of the document must be exactly as given (compute aside: the JS composite also repairs the stale handle, which stale_handle_cases.json pins). An empty `upgraded` means the width rule returns the SAME object. WHAT A SAVED DOCUMENT CANNOT PROVE, so no upgrade claims it: (1) #513 truncated fillOpacity (color.new(c, 87.5) stored as 1-87/100) is the same float as an author's color.new(c, 87); (2) #526 static band default alpha: a band with no fillOpacity drew 0.15, today 1 (v5/v6) or 0.1 (v1-v4) -- the Pine version is not stored (meta.naConditionFalse only says >=4, and only since 2026-10-02) and an authoring set_fill with no opacity writes the identical shape; (3) row linewidth: absent before #513, clamped to 4 between #513 and #526 -- the author's value is not stored; (4) #526 positional fill colour (v4 fill(p1, p2, color.red)): the colour was never stored; (5) #513 condition-column lengths: see memberPaneDefinition.noGuess.test.js; (6) style transp (100-t)/100 vs 1-t/100: transp=90 stored 0.09999999999999998 is the same float as color.new(c, 90), which TradingView paints at byte 25, not 26.",
